<ContentPageLayout title="Website and product privacy" eyebrow="Data and connections" description="What Envarium stores locally, which optional features send data, and what to review before sharing diagnostics.">
  <section class="website panel"><h2>Visiting this website</h2><p>This site includes no behavioral analytics, advertising trackers, or account system. Search runs in your browser using a downloaded documentation index. Your color theme is saved in local storage. Cloudflare handles delivery and security and may process request metadata such as your IP address. GitHub links take you to a separate service with its own privacy policy.</p><a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy →</a></section>
  <div class="principles"><section class="panel"><span>01</span><h2>Project state is stored locally.</h2><p>Project definitions, operation history, settings, and retained logs use local storage. Workloads, plugins, and external coding clients can access the network under your OS account; their behavior is outside Envarium’s data-sharing controls.</p></section><section class="panel"><span>02</span><h2>Review AI evidence before sending.</h2><p>Envarium-assisted onboarding and diagnosis preview a bounded, redacted payload that can include file excerpts. Confirm the provider request before dispatch. Provider terms and retention apply afterward. External coding clients have their own sharing controls.</p></section><section class="panel"><span>03</span><h2>Usage metrics are opt-in.</h2><p>Metrics are off by default, with no default destination. Enabling them requires confirmation and an HTTPS endpoint you choose. While enabled, Envarium periodically sends counters, a random installation ID, version, OS, architecture, and generation time.</p></section></div>
  <section class="connections panel"><h2>Optional connections also share data.</h2><p>Paired peers can exchange the allowlisted project and environment metadata described in the federation guide. Team-sync files carry verified portable configuration and publisher records. Review what each feature shares before enabling a connection or exporting a file.</p><nav><a href="/docs/federation/">Peer sharing →</a><a href="/docs/team-configuration/">Team configuration →</a><a href="/docs/privacy/">Complete data-sharing contract →</a></nav><p>Metrics payloads contain no project paths, commands, logs, or error details. The installation ID links payloads from the same installation, and the receiving endpoint can see network metadata. Disabling metrics clears that ID and pending counters.</p></section>
  <section class="data panel"><div><p class="eyebrow">Support bundles</p><h2>Preview before sharing.</h2><p>Bundles include build and configuration facts and recent redacted internal warnings and errors. They are written locally and are not uploaded automatically.</p><a href="/docs/support-bundles/">Preview and create a support bundle →</a></div><div><p>The bundle builder excludes:</p><ul><li>Repository source files and database contents</li><li>Resolved credentials and raw environment values</li><li>Terminal output and project application log streams</li></ul></div></section>
  <p class="review">Redaction cannot recognize every secret or personal value. Inspect previews and files before sharing them. Report security concerns through <a href="https://github.com/dcuellar322/envarium/security/advisories/new">private vulnerability reporting</a>.</p>
</ContentPageLayout>

